  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 AM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nWednesday through Thursday...\n\nThe center of the surface high pressure has moved east of the \nforecast area bringing return flow back to the region. This is \nbringing warmer temperatures and moisture into the Ohio Valley. \nDaytime temperatures will continue to increase over the next couple \ndays, with todays highs in the mid to upper 80s. The Bowling Green \narea has the best chance of warming to 90 degrees briefly today. \nCloud cover that is currently moving in from the west could limit \nsome daytime heating preventing highs from reaching near 90.\nNighttime lows will cool into the mid and upper 60s tonight as skies \nbecome mostly clear. As upper level ridging holds on over the region \nfor Thursday, temperatures will continue to increase with the strong \nsouthwesterly flow. Max temperatures will warm into the upper 80s \nwith a some areas of low 90s. \n\nThursday Night through Early Next Week...\n\nBy Thursday night, a cold front will be approaching the area from \nthe northwest. In combination with the ample moisture advection, \nwidespread rain showers are expected to develop ahead of the \nboundary and move through the Ohio Valley. Showers will likely start \nlate Thursday/early Friday and linger through majority of the day on \nFriday. Severe threat with this system is low as instability will be \nlimited by the lack of heating at that point. Additionally, wind \nshear values are marginal ahead of the frontal boundary. Locally \nheavy rainfall will be possible across the area as PWATs are \nexpected to be aorund 2 inches. QPF values are ranging 0.5\" \n- 1.5\" with the higher amounts in south central KY. As the cold \nfront pushes through on Friday, maximum temperature will take a dive \nback into the 70s. Depending of the position of the boundary during \npeak heating on Friday, the southern part of the CWA may still reach \nthe low to mid 80s. \n\nRecent guidance has shown a drier trend for the weekend rather than \nlingering rain showers. However, there is still potential for \nshowers on Saturday depending on how fast, or slow, the cold front \nmoves through the area. The 00z GFS guidance is showing rain showers \nout by late Friday, while the euro has training showers along the \nKY/TN border. Conditions for the weekend look pleasant otherwise \nwith maximum temperatures in the mid to low 70s. Nighttime \ntemperatures will cool into the mid 50s on Saturday, and may dip \ninto the upper 40s on Sunday with some clearing skies. \n\nPost frontal boundary an area of high pressure will build back into \nthe region. Therefore, northeasterly flow returns with drier air \nbeing advected into the Ohio Valley. This will keep temperatures in \nthe 70s for the beginning of the work week.",
